<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michel Le Tellier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1603">1603</a>–<a href="/years/1685">1685</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michel Le Tellier was a French statesman who served as secretary of state for war from 1643 to 1677 and later as chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He began the reform of the French army, bringing its officers and supply under royal control, a work continued by his son, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-michel-le-tellier-marquis-de-louvois">Marquis de Louvois</a>. As chancellor he sealed the revocation of the Edict of Nantes in 1685, shortly before his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-le-tellier.jpg" alt="Michel Le Tellier" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Robert Nanteuil / Formerly attributed to Ferdinand Elle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michel-le-tellier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
